by Spomky at 2026-09-06T19:12:18Z

The date claims are now fail-closed, so that judgement call goes your way.

`is_numeric()` guarded the check rather than the value, so `exp: "not-a-number"`, `exp: []` and `nbf: "soon"` skipped it entirely and the token was granted. RFC 7662 §2.2 types the three members as integer timestamps, so one carrying anything else describes no instant a resource server can confront its clock with. `OidcTokenHandler` being fail-closed on the same input settles it: two handlers in one directory should not read a malformed `exp` two ways.

The three sites go through a `readTimestamp()` that returns null on an absent member and throws on a present one that is not numeric. A JSON null is still read as absent, which §2.2 allows. Four rows in `unusableTokens` cover the malformed shapes and fail on the previous handler, and two tests pin what stays granted, `exp: null` and a stringly typed `exp: "1719000001"`, so nobody tightens `is_numeric` into `is_int` later.

